{"results":[{"url":"https://statecourtreport.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/mail-voting-restrictions-face-state-constitutional-challenges","final_url":"https://statecourtreport.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/mail-voting-restrictions-face-state-constitutional-challenges","title":"Mail Voting Restrictions Face State Constitutional Challenges","description":"Courts in multiple states have considered cases about the constitutionality of state laws governing ballot defects, late-arriving ballots, and ID requirements for mail voters.","language":"en","text":"# Mail Voting Restrictions Face State Constitutional Challenges\n\nCourts in multiple states have considered cases about the constitutionality of state laws governing ballot defects, late-arriving ballots, and ID requirements for mail voters.\n\nBy\nKathrina Szymborski Wolfkot,\n\nSarah Kessler\n\n**Published:** October 30, 2026\n\nMail and absentee voting has been the subject of controversy since President Trump — without evidence — declared that those ballots are prone to fraud and promised to “lead a movement” to eliminate them before this year’s midterms. (Trump voted by mail in Florida this year.)\n\nPerhaps unsurprisingly, states reacted in recent years by passing laws restricting who can vote absentee or by mail, and how. Many of these laws have been challenged in state courts. The cases frequently raise state constitutional claims; almost every state constitution contains explicit protections for voting rights and about half mandate that elections be “free,” “equal,” or “open.”\n\nIn the months leading to this year’s midterm elections, cases about the constitutionality of mail and absentee rules across the country centered on ballot defects, late-arriving ballots, and ID requirements for people voting absentee. The results have been mixed, with some courts striking down restrictive laws and others upholding them.\n\n### **Defects on Ballots**\n\nThe Michigan Supreme Court on Thursday left in place an intermediate court holding that state law does not require rejection of absentee ballots with missing stub numbers or with stub numbers that do not match the identifying number printed on the ballot return envelope. The ruling leaves in effect guidance from the secretary of state directing that such ballots be tabulated as challenged ballots. Justice Brian Zahra dissented; he would have granted leave to review the case because of its statewide and national stakes.\n\nThe appellate court decision in the case, *Republican National Committee v. Secretary of State*, noted a state constitutional clause providing the right to vote by absentee ballot and addressing deficiencies in those ballots reinforced its “reluctance to infer a mandatory-rejection rule that the legislature did not state.”\n\nEarlier this month, a Montana trial court enjoined a law requiring voters to put their birth year on their absentee ballot envelope and requiring that year to match the voter’s registration and absentee ballot application in order for the ballot to count. Although the court in *Disability Rights Montana v. State* found the law imposed only a minimal burden on voting — it did not prevent anyone from casting a ballot, it said — the court nonetheless held that it violated the state constitutional right to vote.\n\nThe court analyzed the law using a more protective framework than the federal sliding-scale test for laws burdening voting rights. Voting rights experts have called the federal *Anderson-Burdick* test “relatively feeble.” High courts in other states, including Massachusetts and Washington, have also rejected the framework in favor of more protective tests to assess restrictions on voting rights.\n\nUnder Montana’s test, the state needed to show that the statute was reasonable and served a legitimate state interest that was more important than the burden on the right to vote. The court found the state failed that test because it offered no proof for its assertion that “requiring a birth year reduces the risk that a ballot is returned by someone other than the voter,” and no explanation for how requiring a birth year raises public confidence in elections.\n\nLast week, a Kansas trial court resolved a similar issue, deciding that a statutory provision requiring election officials not to count mail-in ballots that lack a signature or have a signature mismatch did not violate state equal protection or due process. The judge found that evidence from a July trial demonstrated that officials had implemented the signature verification requirement with objective criteria and reasonable uniformity statewide, and that voters had adequate opportunities to cure any discrepancies with their signatures.\n\nThe case, *League of Women Voters of Kansas v. Schwab*, was on remand from the Kansas Supreme Court, which held the statute did not violate the state constitutional right to vote. That high court decision in the case made headlines for its declaration that the right to vote was not fundamental.\n\n### **Late-arriving Ballots**\n\nSeveral recent cases concerned rules around late-arriving absentee or mail ballots. The U.S. Supreme Court ruled in June that federal election statutes do not preclude states from counting mail ballots that arrive after Election Day but did not address whether states may enact policies preventing such ballots from being counted.\n\nA trial judge in Wisconsin this month orally denied a temporary injunction against a state election commission policy that bars voters from casting provisional ballots in person on Election Day if they are concerned their mailed ballots may arrive after 8:00 pm that day — the deadline for being counted. The plaintiff, the League of Women Voters, argued the policy creates a risk of disenfranchisement, particularly given Postal Service delays, that violates the state constitutional right to vote. The case is ongoing.\n\nIn a case raising similar issues, *Kansas Appleseed Center for Law and Justice v. Schwab*, a Kansas trial court sided with the plaintiffs, issuing a temporary injunction in July against a law that would disqualify mail ballots that arrive at election offices after 7:00 pm on Election Day. The ruling means a prior law, which requires officials to count mail ballots postmarked on or before Election Day so long as received within three days, will be in place this election cycle. The court found the plaintiffs were substantially likely to prevail on their claims that the law violated state constitutional rights to vote, equal protection, and due process. The court said the law created an unreasonable burden on the right to vote by requiring “any mail-in ballot to survive a difficult-to-meet and unforgiving deadline, with no ability to correct the deficiency.” It also subjected mail ballots to arbitrary treatment, according to the court, as they will be rejected “due to postal delays outside the voters’ control” and the impact “will be non-uniformly spread across the state.”\n\nThe Kansas Supreme Court refused to review the intermediate court’s denial of a stay of injunction. In doing so, the court rejected the secretary of state’s argument that the injunction violated the *Purcell* principle — a doctrine that counsels courts to avoid changing the election rules too close to an election — by disrupting the status quo within weeks of the state’s August primary. The majority called the secretary and dissent’s reliance on the principle “problematic” and said recent rulings from the U.S. Supreme Court show an “inconsistency” in application of the principle.\n\nThis criticism echoes election law scholars: “The Supreme Court has invoked *Purcell* some nine months before the election,” Cardozo Law Professor Wilfred U. Codrington III has written, “yet has refused to adhere to it even when voting had already started.” Moreover, because the principle applies only to courts, Codrington continued, it “incentivizes elected officials and their co-partisans to change rules to their benefit, including on the eve of elections — and even when voting is already underway.”\n\n### **ID Requirements for Absentee Voting**\n\nLate last year, a New Hampshire lower court dismissed a challenge brought by three legally blind voters, under the state’s Free Elections Clause, to a 2025 law that creates strict identification requirements for absentee voters. New Hampshire citizens who want to vote absentee already had to apply anew in each election, but under the law they must also now submit a copy of an approved photo ID with their application, travel to the clerk’s office to present such ID, or have their signature on the application form notarized.\n\nApplying precedent from the New Hampshire Supreme Court adopting the *Anderson-Burdick* framework, the trial court in *Robertson v. Secretary of State* concluded the law’s restriction of the right to vote was reasonable and non-discriminatory.\n\nWhile the trial court’s decision means the law is in effect for the midterms, the state high court is set to hear oral arguments in the case on November 23. On appeal, the plaintiffs argue the lower court disregarded their detailed factual allegations that the requirements impose “a severe burden for many voters” and “are harder for blind voters like them.” They also say the court’s conclusion that “any state interest justified the burdens” was wrong, especially given the “absence of any evidence that New Hampshire had any meaningful problem with voters requesting absentee ballots in other voters’ names.” In their response, state officials contend, in part, that there is no fundamental right to vote absentee under the New Hampshire Constitution, so rational basis — an even less protective standard than *Anderson-Burdick* — should apply.\n\n## **• • •**\n\nBecause state law — not federal — sets the rules around how and when elections take place, state courts hear the majority of election-related litigation across the country. With mail and absentee voting a political flashpoint, state constitutions and the courts that interpret them are among the most crucial defense against attacks on our right to vote.\n\n*Kathrina Szymborski Wolfkot is the managing editor of* State Court Report *and a senior counsel and manager in the Judiciary Program at the Brennan Center for Justice.*\n\n*Sarah Kessler is an advisor and contributing editor to* State Court Report*.*\n\nSuggested Citation: Kathrina Szymborski Wolfkot & Sarah Kessler, *Mail Voting Restrictions Face State Constitutional Challenges*, Sᴛᴀᴛᴇ Cᴏᴜʀᴛ Rᴇᴘᴏʀᴛ (Oct. 30, 2026), https://statecourtreport.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/mail-voting-restrictions-face-state-constitutional-challenges\n\n## Related Commentary\n\n* Democrats and liberal candidates won in Montana, North Carolina, Ohio, and Texas, among other key races.\n\n  By\n  Alicia Bannon,\n\n  Douglas Keith\n* In Idaho, a ballot measure that would have guaranteed reproductive freedom and privacy failed.\n\n  By\n  Mary Ziegler\n* ## What to Know On Election Day\n\n  From state supreme court races to constitutional amendments, from court disputes over polling hours to laws mandating certification, here’s what voters should understand for going to the polls and what happens after.\n\n  By\n  Alicia Bannon","author":null,"published_date":"2026-10-30T12:00:00Z","latency_ms":380.3276429998732,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}